WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
 Bash, a command language interpreter for interacting with a computer from the command line.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.380
 It's also called a shell because it surrounds the operating system kernel to hide its intricate details,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:17.000
 while allowing you, the programmer, to do important stuff like access data and write files by typing simple commands.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.760
 This was a revolutionary concept when it was developed in the early 70s, back when programmers were still using punch cards.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.020
 The shell concept evolved over the years, with the born shell being the most popular version.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:31.140
 That is, until 1989, when the born-again shell, or Bash, came about.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:38.320
 When you open up the terminal on a Unix machine, like macOS and most Linux distros, the default shell is usually Bash.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.580
 It provides a prompt where you can type a command, which will then be interpreted by the shell and executed on the operating system.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.020
 To find out if you're running Bash, type in which $shell from the command line.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.320
 It's like any other application that lives in the binaries directory.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:01:00.160
 But Bash is also a programming language that allows us to write scripts, which means anything we type manually into the command line can be automated with code.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:07.580
 When you first launch the shell, it actually runs a startup script that's defined in the Bash profile or Bash RC file on your system.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:12.280
 This allows you to customize the behavior and appearance of the shell whenever you start a new session.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:19.340
 You can add your own custom Bash scripts to any project by creating a file that ends in .sh or no file extension at all.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:24.880
 The first line in that file should always be a shebang, followed by the path to the application that should run it.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:30.340
 Below that, we can start writing commands, like echo to print something, and they'll be interpreted line by line.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:37.780
 To create a variable, type a name in all caps, followed by the equal sign, then reference it later in the script using a dollar sign in front of the name.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:41.420
 Now to execute the script, simply type the file name into the shell.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:45.660
 That was easy, but what if we want to pass in some arguments when we run the script?

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.860
 Positional arguments will automatically be assigned variable names of 1, 2, 3, and so on.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.900
 Now in other cases, you may need additional user input in the middle of a script.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:02.820
 You can create loops in Bash, like a do-while loop here, that will prompt the user to continue the script on a yes answer or exit on a no answer.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:10.240
 From there we can implement conditional logic with an if statement, which will test if the value on the left side is less than the value on the right side.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.920
 If true, then run this command, otherwise run the else command.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:21.740
 Another cool feature is that if you have multiple long running processes, you can run them in parallel in the background by adding an ampersand after the command.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:25.040
 This has been Bash, the born-again shell, in 100 seconds.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.960
 If you want to see more short videos like this, make sure to hit the like button and subscribe.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.860
 Thanks for watching, and I will see you in the next one.

